---
// Utils
import { Picture, getImage } from "astro:assets";

// Components
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Banner from "@components/Banner/Banner.astro";
import Stats from "@components/Stats/stats.astro";
import CTA from "@components/CTA/CTASimple.astro";

// Images
import landingImage from "@assets/images/landing.jpg"; // <-- THE PATH TO THE ASSET YOU WANT TO PRELOAD - The asset must live in src
const optimizedImage = await getImage({ src: landingImage, format: "webp" });

import FenceWashIMG1 from "@assets/images/services/fenceWash1.jpeg";
import FenceWashIMG2 from "@assets/images/services/fenceWash2.jpeg";
import FenceWashIMG3 from "@assets/images/services/fenceWash3.jpeg";
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Fence Washing in Jacksonville, FL | Pristine Patio Pros"
	description="Professional fence cleaning in Jacksonville. Pressure washing & soft washing for wood, vinyl & composite fences. Restore your fence. Free quotes available!"
	heroImage={optimizedImage}
>
	<Banner title="Fence Washing" image={optimizedImage} />

	<!-- SBS 1 -->
	<section id="sbs-2316">
		<div class="cs-container">
			<div class="cs-content cs-content1">
				<Picture
					src={FenceWashIMG1}
					alt="wooden patio porch being pressured washed"
					width={608}
					formats={["avif", "webp"]}
					pictureAttributes={{ class: "cs-picture cs-picture1" }}
				/>
				<div class="cs-wrapper cs-wrapper1">
					<h1 class="cs-title">Fence Washing in Jacksonville, FL</h1>
					<p class="cs-text">
						Fences are exposed to constant weather and moisture, and in
						Jacksonville's climate that means streaks, algae, and general fading
						build up faster than most homeowners notice until it becomes hard to
						ignore. Fence washing addresses that buildup across the full
						surface, bringing back a more consistent color along long runs of
						fencing and clearing out the algae lines and streaking that moisture
						leaves behind. The result is a cleaner, more uniform appearance
						around the perimeter of the property that makes the whole yard feel
						more put together. It is also worth noting that organic growth left
						on fencing long enough will start to work against the material
						itself, whether that is wood, vinyl, or chain link. Keeping the
						surface clean is one of the simpler ways to slow that process down
						and get more life out of the fence.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- 3-Card -->
	<section id="services-1331">
		<div class="cs-container">
			<h2 class="cs-h2">Fence Types Typically Cleaned</h2>
			<ul class="cs-card-group">
				<li class="cs-item">
					<div class="cs-link">
						<h3 class="cs-h3">Wood Fencing</h3>
						<p class="cs-item-text">
							Wood fencing is one of the more common types found around
							Jacksonville homes, and it tends to collect mold, mildew, and
							general discoloration fairly quickly in this climate. Moisture
							gets trapped in the grain and along the posts, creating the kind
							of buildup that makes a fence look far older than it actually is.
							Fence washing removes that organic growth and restores a cleaner,
							more natural appearance to the wood. Low pressure and appropriate
							cleaning solutions are used to get the job done without damaging
							the material.
						</p>
					</div>
				</li>
				<li class="cs-item">
					<div class="cs-link">
						<h3 class="cs-h3">Vinyl and PVC Fencing</h3>
						<p class="cs-item-text">
							Vinyl fencing is popular for its durability, but that doesn't mean
							it stays clean on its own. Green algae and general grime tend to
							cling to the surface over time, especially on the shaded side of
							the fence. A soft wash approach works well here since vinyl
							responds better to low pressure and cleaning solution than it does
							to a direct high-pressure blast. The result is a fence that looks
							closer to the bright, clean appearance it had when it was first
							installed.
						</p>
					</div>
				</li>
				<li class="cs-item">
					<div class="cs-link">
						<h3 class="cs-h3">Chain Link Fencing</h3>
						<p class="cs-item-text">
							Chain link fencing might seem low maintenance, but dirt, rust
							staining, and organic growth build up in the links and along the
							posts over time. In Jacksonville's humidity that process moves
							faster than most homeowners expect, leaving the fence looking worn
							and neglected even when the rest of the property is well kept.
							Pressure washing clears out the buildup that collects in and
							around the links and gives the fence a cleaner overall appearance.
							It is one of the quicker services to complete but makes a
							noticeable difference in how the whole yard looks.
						</p>
					</div>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>

	<Stats />

	<!-- SBS 2 -->
	<section id="sbs-1565">
		<div class="cs-container">
			<Picture
				src={FenceWashIMG3}
				alt="wooden patio porch being pressured washed"
				width={586}
				formats={["avif", "webp"]}
				pictureAttributes={{ class: "cs-picture" }}
			/>
			<div class="cs-content">
				<span class="cs-topper">How fence cleaning works</span>
				<h2 class="cs-title">Fence Cleaning Method</h2>
				<ul class="cs-card-group">
					<li class="cs-li">
						<div class="cs-flex">
							<h3 class="cs-h3">Walk Fence Line Carefully</h3>
							<p class="cs-li-text">
								Fence lines are walked so the material, hardware, worn areas,
								and overgrown vegetation are identified. Sections that may not
								respond well to higher pressure are noted in advance so they can
								be treated more gently. Loose debris at the base of the fence,
								such as leaves and grass, is cleared where practical.
								Landscaping, decor, and nearby property are considered so
								cleaning does not create unnecessary disturbance along the fence
								line.
							</p>
						</div>
					</li>
					<li class="cs-li">
						<div class="cs-flex">
							<h3 class="cs-h3">Select Settings and Cleaner</h3>
							<p class="cs-li-text">
								Pressure levels and cleaning solutions are chosen based on
								whether the fence is vinyl, painted, or another approved
								surface. The goal is to loosen algae, mildew, and surface
								buildup while helping reduce the risk of stripping or damage.
							</p>
						</div>
					</li>
					<li class="cs-li">
						<div class="cs-flex">
							<h3 class="cs-h3">Clean Sections and Rinse</h3>
							<p class="cs-li-text">
								The fence is cleaned in manageable sections so coverage remains
								even on both sides where possible. Rinsing follows the cleaning
								passes so dirty water runs away from the fence, and lines or
								streaks are checked from common viewing angles.
							</p>
						</div>
					</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<CTA />
</BaseLayout>

